tester.evidence:
1. Source file verification: `cvw/src/privileged/trap.sv` lines 120-123 unambiguously read:
```systemverilog
    else if (StoreAmoPageFaultM)                              CauseM = 5'd15;
    else if (LoadPageFaultM)                                  CauseM = 5'd13;
    else if (StoreAmoAccessFaultM)                            CauseM = 5'd7;
    else if (LoadAccessFaultM)                                CauseM = 5'd5;
```
This contradicts the architect's claim of an ascending numeric sort (`4 -> 5 -> 6 -> 7 -> 13 -> 15`) and proves the priority logic correctly favors Page Faults.
2. Negative finding reproducer: Executing `tester_llm/20260913T002730Z-c0bd0ca4/reproducer.sh` builds and runs the test suite. S-mode attempts to access virtual address `0x4000`, which incurs both a TLB miss (due to `V=0` in the identity-mapped root PTE) and a PMP default-deny Access Fault. As expected, `test_a` (Load) traps with `scause=13` and `test_b` (Store) traps with `scause=15` on both Spike and the unmodified Wally RTL, passing the assertions and exiting cleanly with code 0.
3. Misaligned access compliance: Lines 124-125 of `trap.sv` place Misaligned Faults (Causes 4 and 6) after Access Faults (Causes 5 and 7) ONLY when `P.ZICCLSM_SUPPORTED` is true. This correctly adheres to Section 3.1.15 of the specification, which dictates that if hardware misaligned access is supported but interrupted by a page/access fault, the page/access fault takes precedence.
